## =============================================================================
## Adding things, and where a fact came from (v0.2.5)
## =============================================================================

feat:AddMenu
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "The add menu" ;
    skos:definition     "The + button at the top of every page opens a short menu of what you can add. Under New: a source (a whole document), a single statement, a set or a new space. Under From: a note, a link, a folder, a code repository, a calendar, a reminder or a space file. Each choice opens the right page already set up for it. The arrow keys move through the menu and Escape closes it." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Ingest ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:add-quick-menu ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:add-quick-menu .

feat:NodeSources
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Where a node's facts came from" ;
    skos:definition     "Select a node and its details now show which sources its facts came from. Under each source are the facts that source gave, so you can see at a glance what came from where. Click a source's title to open it in Sources, where it is selected for you and any filter that would hide it is cleared. Facts are listed whichever direction they point, even when the Statements filters would hide them." ;
    skos:broader        feat:PassageGrounding ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:statement-node-source-links ;
    skos:broadMatch     kb:source-atoms ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:statement-node-source-links ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:source-atoms .

## =============================================================================
## The evidence chain: a fact points at the words it came from (v0.2.6)
## =============================================================================

feat:OpenInSource
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:statement-to-passage-jump ;
    rdfs:label          "Open a fact's source at the exact words" ;
    skos:definition     "When Reckons.AI reads a source and proposes a fact, the fact records exactly which stretch of text it came from: the passage, where the quote starts and ends, and a fingerprint of the source's text at that moment. Open a fact's Evidence and press Open in source: the source opens, scrolls to the passage, and the quoted words are marked. If the quote cannot be found in the text that was kept, there is no button, and the Evidence section says why. If the source's text has changed since the fact was taken from it, the evidence is marked stale, either because the source changed or because the passage moved. A stale link is never quietly pointed at different words." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "TEXT ONLY. A fact can point at a passage of text, not at a page of a PDF, a box on a page or a region of an image. Those kinds of anchor are planned and not built." ;
    skos:broader        feat:PassageGrounding ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:statement-to-passage-jump ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:anchor-contract ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:anchor-staleness .

feat:SpaceFilePassages
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:chunk-space-folder ;
    rdfs:label          "A space file that carries its evidence" ;
    skos:definition     "A space's file now holds the passages its facts came from, in their own section after the facts. Each fact's link to its words is written too, in the W3C Web Annotation form, so other tools can read it. Copy that one file to another computer or another person, import it, and Open in source still lands on the same words. Saving, sync, Google Drive backups, auto-save and export all write the passages; an import checks each passage against its fingerprint and never overwrites one that is already there. Files from before this change still import as they did." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "ORIGINALS STAY BESIDE THE FILE. The passages travel inside the space's file; the original documents they were cut from are still kept next to it, not inside the space's folder. Moving them is planned." ;
    skos:broader        feat:TTLExport ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:chunk-importer-parse ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:chunk-section-export ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:chunk-space-folder ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:anchor-roundtrip .

feat:Proposals
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:broadMatch     kb:graph-proposals ;
    rdfs:label          "Proposals: decisions that cite their evidence" ;
    skos:definition     "A proposal is how Reckons.AI will put a decision in front of you: two to four options, each listing the facts it rests on, and through those facts the passages they came from, so you can follow any option back to its evidence before you choose. The shape of a proposal is now defined and checked: what a valid one must contain, the reasons an invalid one is refused, and the stages it moves through from suggested to decided." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "A DEFINITION ONLY. Nothing in the app makes, stores or shows a proposal yet. Building the options with their citations, and the screen to review them, are planned." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:proposal-shape .

feat:StarterReviewed
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:starter-provenance ;
    rdfs:label          "Getting started arrives already reviewed" ;
    skos:definition     "The Getting started space is a small made-up story: two friends, Alex and Jordan, planning a camping weekend. It comes with seven short sample sources (a text thread, a voice memo, notes on each campground, a forecast and route notes), each opening with a line that says it is a sample. Its 48 facts each show the passage they came from, who accepted them and when, including two corrections and two rejections, so the review history you see is a real one. Three facts are left for you: the Review tab opens with them waiting for your decision." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:starter-provenance .

## =============================================================================
## Spaces you can stand inside (v0.2.6)
## =============================================================================

feat:Splats
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:splat-node-view ;
    rdfs:label          "3D captures on a node" ;
    skos:definition     "A node can carry a Gaussian splat: a 3D object or scene drawn as many small, soft points of color. In the 3D graph the splat is drawn at the node, and the nodes, lines and labels behind it are hidden, so it reads as a solid thing. Open it in the viewer to turn it and look from any side. Pictures, clips, 3D models and splats are built-in types (Image, Video, 3D model, Gaussian splat), alongside Prompt and License. How an asset was made, and under which license, shows in its details rather than as extra nodes, while the link to what it was made from stays a visible line." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "3D ONLY. The 2D view does not draw splats. Hiding what sits behind a splat was measured in software rendering, not on a real graphics card. Image, Video and License share a shape with other types and are told apart by color and icon." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-node-view ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-occlusion ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:media-entity-types .

feat:SpaceBackground
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:splat-space-background ;
    rdfs:label          "A background for a space" ;
    skos:definition     "A space can have a background behind its 3D graph. Choose Set as background on a node that carries a splat, or import a capture from your device (.ply, .spz or .sog files; it warns above 50 MB and refuses above 300 MB, and the file is stored on your device, never copied into the space's text). Then open View settings, the panel at the top left of the graph: choose whether the background sits behind the graph or all around you, turn it, change its size, position and opacity, or level its floor. Place in space puts a node's splat where you want it inside the background, at the size you want, without the large preview covering the graph. A scene from threejs.org can be a background too: it never loads until you press Load, runs in a sealed frame, and can be stopped from View settings or from the bar on the graph. A space has one background at a time." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "A Three.js scene that crashes the browser's graphics driver can still take the page down; the sealed frame keeps it away from your data, not from the driver." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-space-background ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-background-settings ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-file-import ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-placement ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:background-in-view ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:threejs-example-background .

feat:YourRoom
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:room-scene-space ;
    rdfs:label          "The Your room example space" ;
    skos:definition     "Your room is an example space that walks through four steps: scan a room, import the capture, generate an object, and place it in the room. It opens from About, under example data. Its room is a reconstruction of a computer-made scene, with a carved turtle placed inside it." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "A GUIDED EXAMPLE, NOT A FINISHED FEATURE. The room is not a real scan, and the placement steps are not covered by a browser test on phones." ;
    skos:broader        feat:SpaceBackground ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:room-scene-space .

feat:RoomScan
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:room-scan-capture ;
    rdfs:label          "Turn a phone scan of a room into a splat, on your own machine" ;
    skos:definition     "With the generation tools installed (see Generation tools and setup), npm run scan:room -- <folder or video> turns a phone recording of a room into a splat: it pulls frames from the video, works out where the camera was for each one, and trains the splat. The result and a receipt stay on your machine, outside the project. npm run scan:quality says how good a recording is (too fast, too shaky, too dark, parts of the room not covered) with one plain tip for each problem. npm run splat:compress makes a splat much smaller in the SPZ or SOG format, checks the copy against the original, and records that the copy is lossy." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "MEASURED ON ONE MACHINE. A 300-frame phone recording took about 19 minutes on one Linux computer with an NVIDIA RTX 3090. It runs on Linux only, the room's scale is arbitrary, and the quality tips use first-guess thresholds from one private scan." ;
    skos:broader        feat:GenerationSetup ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:room-scan-capture ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:scan-quality ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-compression .

feat:ReckoningSimulator
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:sim-core ;
    rdfs:label          "The reckoning simulator, first part" ;
    skos:definition     "A reckoning will be able to try a proposal in a physical scene before you choose it. The first part reads a scene from the graph, steps it with a physics engine, and checks it against conditions such as 'the ball ends in the cup'. npm run sim:run runs the example in static/starter-sim-ramp-cup.ttl, which fails as written and passes once the ball starts higher on the ramp." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "COMMAND LINE ONLY. Nothing in the app uses it yet; trying proposals in a scene and replaying them in the 3D view are planned." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:sim-core .


feat:GenerationSetup rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Generation tools and setup" ;
    skos:exactMatch kb:generated-assets-story ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:generated-assets-story ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shelly-generation-jobs ;
    skos:broader feat:StorySystem ;
    skos:definition """The Generated assets story works in two layers. Viewing existing assets is included. Creating new files uses optional tools on your own generation machine.

| Capability | Available with the app | Additional setup |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Explore example images, videos and Gaussian splats | Bundled examples and viewers | None |
| View/import images, videos, GLB models and splats | Browser asset viewers | Your own compatible files |
| Prompt to image | Story interface | stable-diffusion.cpp with Z-Image-Turbo weights and the local runner |
| Image to video | Story interface | stable-diffusion.cpp with Wan2.2 TI2V-5B weights and the local runner |
| Image to Gaussian splat | Story interface | TripoSplat, Python/CUDA dependencies and the local runner |
| Generate GLB meshes or other derivations | Not implemented in this story | A future adapter; importing/viewing GLB does not generate one |
| Install tools/plugins through an agent | No installer bundled in the story | An external agent you configure and explicitly authorize |

### Set up local generation

1. Use a checkout of Reckons.AI with its npm dependencies installed. The current installer was tested on Ubuntu 24.04 with NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPUs; other operating systems and GPUs are unverified. It uses Linux x86-64 binaries, Vulkan for image/video, and Python 3.11 with CUDA for splats. Install prerequisites including Node.js, git, curl, unzip, tar and uv before running it. Allow about 27 GB for tools/model weights plus space for outputs; this is a download, not a small browser plugin.
2. Read [the setup script](https://github.com/Data-Insight-Solutions/Reckons.AI/blob/dev/scripts/content/gen-setup.sh), then run `bash scripts/content/gen-setup.sh`. It installs tools and downloads models outside the checkout, under `~/.local/share/reckons/gen` by default. Set `RECKONS_GEN_HOME` to choose another location and use the same value for the runner.
3. Choose a private workspace folder outside a Git checkout, or an ignored workspace folder. From the checkout, run `npm run gen:serve -- --workspace=/path/to/your/private-workspace`. Keep this process running. It accepts only image, video and splat requests and writes private job receipts.
4. In desktop Chrome or Edge, open Generated assets, choose **Create an asset with Shelly**, and connect that same folder. This file-queue adapter does not connect directly to a remote SSH server or work in browsers without folder access.
5. Enter an image prompt. The runner checks the output before Shelly adds it for review. After the image arrives, choose a video and describe its motion, or choose a Gaussian splat. A splat invents unseen geometry; it is not a measured scan.

The connected runner reports which stages have their tool and model files installed. Installed files do not guarantee enough GPU memory or a compatible driver; runtime failures are shown. Prompts, requests, receipts and outputs remain in `.reckons-generation` in the private workspace. Stop the runner with Ctrl+C or by closing its terminal. Closing Shelly leaves an active job running; Cancel generation prevents attachment and asks the runner to stop the process. If a runner was killed without stopping cleanly, the next one to start takes over its lock once it finds that runner is gone; it refuses while another runner is still running on the same workspace.

### Tools and integration guides

- [stable-diffusion.cpp](https://github.com/leejet/stable-diffusion.cpp) and [Z-Image-Turbo weights](https://huggingface.co/leejet/Z-Image-Turbo-GGUF).
- [Wan2.2 weights](https://huggingface.co/QuantStack/Wan2.2-TI2V-5B-GGUF).
- [TripoSplat](https://github.com/VAST-AI-Research/TripoSplat).
- [Agent setup and permissions](/docs/features/agent-setup).
- [Integration settings](/settings/integrations), [MCP setup](/docs/integrations-tech/mcp-server), and [Ollama setup](/docs/integrations-tech/ollama).

A local language model does not automatically supply image/video/splat generation. Those are distinct tools with separate model files. The universal integration/plugin SDK and in-app agent management are planned; this guide does not imply that either is available today.""" .

feat:AgentSetup rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Agent setup and installation permissions" ;
    kpred:status-not-applicable "A guide to configuring external agents and granting installation permissions, not an in-app installer." ;
    skos:broader feat:StorySystem ;
    skos:definition """An external coding or automation agent can help install generation tools, plugins, or an optional integration when you give it the appropriate terminal or SSH access. Reckons.AI's browser story does not provide that agent, grant access, install services or hold SSH credentials.

### Start with a limited task

1. Configure your chosen agent using its own setup instructions. If it should read Reckons.AI graphs, connect [the MCP server](/docs/integrations-tech/mcp-server). Reading a graph is separate from permission to run its commands.
2. Give it the specific installation guide and desired result: for example, install the local image/splat tools, configure your own Indico calendar service, or connect your own n8n webhook. Ask for an inspectable plan naming downloads, licenses, disk/GPU requirements, configuration changes, ports and rollback steps.
3. Approve only the necessary effect on the chosen device or server. **Warning: terminal/device control can read or change accessible files, run downloaded code and install software. SSH access extends that authority to the remote account. Elevated privileges can affect the whole machine.** Use a dedicated account with limited permissions, keep backups, and never put passwords, private keys or tokens in a shared graph or prompt example.
4. Let the authorized agent perform the approved installation, then verify the result independently: a checked generated image, a successful read from the configured calendar, or a synthetic webhook test. Review logs and failures. Revoke temporary credentials and remove unneeded privileges when finished.

Reckons.AI includes a developer task runner at `scripts/agent/runner.ts`. For a trusted local task graph, `npx tsx scripts/agent/runner.ts --graph=/path/to/tasks.ttl --dry-run` shows planned work. Its default declared authority is read-only; broader effects must be allowed explicitly. Effect declarations are not an operating-system sandbox. Do not run task graphs supplied by strangers or grant broad effects merely to bypass a failure. The dedicated generation runner is narrower: it accepts fixed generation stages, not graph-authored shell commands.

### Optional service setup

[Indico installation](https://docs.getindico.io/en/stable/installation/) and [n8n hosting](https://docs.n8n.io/deploy/host-n8n/) are service-specific guides. An authorized external agent with appropriate SSH access can follow them on a server you control; server provisioning is not a built-in Reckons.AI feature. Connect the resulting service through [Integrations settings](/settings/integrations). These services are optional and per-user. Reckons.AI remains usable without either server.

For model jobs, see [the local agent tier](/docs/coding-workflow/agent-tier). The broader [portable orchestration design](/docs/coding-workflow/agent-orchestration) and in-app agent dashboard remain planned; they are not prerequisites for an external agent helping you with an approved install.""" .

feat:ThreeExamples rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Three.js examples on nodes" ;
    kpred:page-slug "threejs-examples" ;
    skos:exactMatch kb:threejs-example-nodes ;
    skos:broader feat:StorySystem ;
    skos:definition """Attach an interactive example from [the Three.js gallery](https://threejs.org/examples/) to a node. This opens a separate viewer; it does not turn the example's scene into geometry inside the graph.

1. Select an entity node and find **Three.js example** in its details.
2. Choose **Attach Three.js example**, paste a gallery link such as `https://threejs.org/examples/#webgl_animation_keyframes`, then choose **Attach example**. Direct `https://threejs.org/examples/webgl_animation_keyframes.html` links also work. Only official HTTPS WebGL and WebGPU example links are accepted; XR, arbitrary websites, query options and pasted JavaScript are not supported.
3. Choose **Open example viewer**, then **Load example**. Opening a space, selecting the node or importing its attachment does not execute the example. The explicit load contacts threejs.org and runs its third-party code on your graphics hardware.
4. Use **Stop example** or close the viewer to unload it. A failed example offers **Retry example**. Editing or removing the attachment is available in node details. An attached example takes precedence over that node's other asset previews until removed.

### As the graph background

With an example attached, choose **Set as background** in node details. A bar then appears in the graph view reading "Background: <example name>" with **Load**. Nothing starts until you choose Load, including after a reload or when you open an imported graph. Once running, the bar offers **Interact with background** (sends mouse and touch input to the example until you press the button again or Escape; Escape does not work while the example itself has keyboard focus, so use the button) and **Stop**. A failed background unloads and the bar shows the error and **Retry**. A space has one background: setting an example replaces a background splat, and setting a splat replaces the example.

The background is a separate scene. It does not share the graph's camera, depth or lighting, so nodes float in front of it instead of sitting inside it, and it does not follow the graph camera. It runs a second scene beside the graph, which roughly adds its GPU load to the graph's; stop it on a slow device. The same sandbox and failure-containment limits below apply.

### Loading protection and its limits

The viewer uses a sandboxed frame without same-origin privileges, forms, popups or top-level navigation. The example cannot read Reckons.AI's document or browser storage. Camera, microphone, location, clipboard, fullscreen and XR permissions are not granted. The frame allows scripts and assets from threejs.org; examples requiring other origins or permissions may fail.

Before downloading the example, the frame checks for graphics API availability. It reports script errors, failed asset loads, rejected promises and lost graphics contexts. The parent unloads a scene that has not submitted a GPU draw or WebGPU queue submission within 45 seconds, or whose heartbeat stops for 10 seconds while the app is visible. A document load alone is not success. GPU activity is not a guarantee of correct appearance; examples can still render incorrectly or use too much memory.

**This is failure containment, not guaranteed crash protection.** An iframe is not a dedicated operating-system process. A severe browser, shared rendering-thread or GPU-driver failure can still freeze or crash the tab before its watchdog runs. Use a lighter example if your device struggles. Example compatibility and upstream contents can change.

No local models, plugins or SSH setup are needed for these viewers. They require internet access to threejs.org and compatible browser graphics support. This does not generate or copy an asset into your space; the saved node metadata holds a link. See the [Three.js compatibility guide](https://threejs.org/manual/pages/webgl-compatibility-check.html), and [generation setup](/docs/features/generation-setup) for creating your own image, video or splat.""" .


